 we often want things to  be black and white we  say this is what this is really about  the reality is this is a country of 331  million people we have examples of  everything you can imagine we have  dozens of different problems that relate  to what we are now seeing just this  weekend we saw sickening violence done  by police to protesters including for  example an elderly man with a cane  shoved onto the ground by police and we  saw police and I believe it was Michigan  show up and tell the protesters I'm  putting down my weapons I'm with you  let's march together we saw protesters  burn entire buildings and brutalize  business owners including middle-aged  women and yet we also saw protesters  guard a police officer who was separated  from his squad on Saturday night and  this is the risk about the out of  context sharing of pictures and video on  social media ten seconds from one  protest and 15 from another and and I  actually did some of this over the  weekend and I don't know that it was the  best thing not only does it lack  immediate context it lacks broader  context about all of the issues that  have gotten us to where we are now  including by the way a complete lack of  leadership from the President and we're  going to talk about that later which is  why I'm not digging into it too much  right here so my message is what we are  seeing is mostly about systems and it is  less about individuals you're seeing a  ton of debates about what portion of  police are good versus bad which is very  fraught terminology anyway good good and  bad what portion of protesters have good  or bad intentions what portion of  protesters are grassroots members of the  community versus out-of-town astroturf  furs as has been a growing narrative  over the weekend used in part to  discredit the real and justified anger  that protesters have about what's going  on in their communities the problem is  if you just argue about that if you  spend your time on those questions well  82% 